// Two voices each carrying a dynamic on the same opening note column: the upper
// @f and lower @p must BOTH render (regression guard — they previously drew on
// top of each other so only one showed). They stack vertically.
title "Two Voices Dynamics"
composer "Lily#"
tempo 100
time 4/4
key c major

part m { clef treble }

section Main {
  m { voice { e'4@f f g a | b2 a2 | } { c4@p d e f | g2 f2 | }  }
}

form main { Main }

score main "voice-dynamics" {
  staff m
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// ⚠️ THIS NOTE IS AT THE FILE END ON PURPOSE. A comment ABOVE the music shifts every
// source offset after it, so the whole book's data-pos moves and its snapshot has to be
// re-based -- a diff that proves nothing and hides the next real one. Below the music,
// nothing moves (measured: suite green with these notes, 10 snapshots red without).
// ✔ VERIFIED TO GUARD, not assumed to (session 190): VoiceDefaults.GetDefaultStemUp returning null moved this book (31 of 219 moved). Dropping the dynamics' own skyline contribution (AddDynamicsToSkyline) does NOT move it -- that is voice-dynamics-multistaff's mechanism, not this one's.
